In the time when stars slept late and rivers remembered every footstep, there lived a boy named Aelar who could hear the world’s whisper in the wind. He grew up near a hill crowned with a lone pine that no winter could bare. The elders said the tree kept a secret, but no one spoke of it aloud, for secrets that breathe can ache the heart.

One dusk, as the hill blushed with the first light of the moon, the pine tree spoke in a voice like rain on a tin roof. It told Aelar that the mountains remembered every promise ever made to a seed. If a promise remained unkept, a small part of the world would wither away, like a leaf without a tree to drink the rain.

Aelar wandered through villages, gathering broken vows like shells along a shore. He reminded people of their promises, not with scorn but with the patience of a river learning to bend around stone. When the last vow was mended, the earth exhaled a sigh of green.

From that day, the hill bore a new fragrance—of renewal. The pine whispered that truth, kept tender, can grow into a forest. And so every creature learned to listen—to hearts, to raindrops, to promises—so the world would never forget to be kept.
