In the beginning, there was a quiet lake ringed by Tamarind trees, and in its still waters slept a single silver fish. The fish dreamed in colors no eye could see, and from its dreams sprouted voices—the voices of rivers, winds, and the mountains. They spoke of balance, and of a day when the world would forget to listen.

One evening, a hunter named Nira came to the lake with a bow that never missed. She heard the whispers and believed they were warnings about a great beast to come. But the voices sang of a different danger: complacency. “When you think you can own the sound of the world,” they warned, “you lose the art of listening.”

Nira followed the voices until she found the silver fish. The fish did not flee; it glowed with a pale, patient light and spoke without words, sending a ripple through the air that tasted like rain. It told Nira to plant a seed at the lake’s edge, a seed that would grow only if she learned to hear the smallest breath of life—a leaf trembling, a stone breathing, a root sigh.

Nira planted the seed. Seasons turned, and from the seed rose a tree that bent toward the lake, its branches brushing the water like fingers seeking a pulse. The tree did not demand tribute. It asked only to be listened to. And so the people learned to listen again, and the lake woke with a new song—the world, in balance, remembering to hear.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

